1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,071
Book Three, Proposition Two.

2
00:00:02,071 --> 00:00:09,502
If on the circumference of a circle two points be taken at random, the segment joining the points will fall within the circle.

3
00:00:09,502 --> 00:00:14,810
Let A B C be a circle, and let two points A and B be taken at random on its circumference.

4
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:20,204
I say that the segment joined from A to B will fall within the circle.

5
00:00:20,204 --> 00:00:25,391
For suppose it does not, but, if possible, let it fall outside, as A E B.

6
00:00:25,391 --> 00:00:35,480
Take the center D of the circle A B C by Three One, join D A and D B, and draw D F E through to meet the circumference at F and the line at E.

7
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:42,211
Since the radii D A and D B are equal, by One Five the angles D A E and D B E are equal.

8
00:00:42,211 --> 00:00:53,971
And since one side A E B of triangle D A E is produced, by One Sixteen the exterior angle D E B is greater than angle D A E, and therefore greater than angle D B E.

9
00:00:53,971 --> 00:01:02,146
In triangle D B E, the greater angle is subtended by the greater side by One Nineteen, so D B is greater than D E.

10
00:01:02,146 --> 00:01:10,178
Yet D B equals the radius D F; therefore D F is greater than D E, the less than the greater—which is impossible.

11
00:01:10,178 --> 00:01:17,851
Neither will the segment fall on the circumference itself; therefore the chord A B falls within the circle.

12
00:01:17,851 --> 00:01:19,500
Which was to be proved.

13
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,974
Proposition Three Two establishes the strict convexity of the circle.

14
00:01:23,974 --> 00:01:40,659
As Heath and De Morgan note, the same exterior-angle chain proves directly that every interior point of a chord lies closer to the center than the radius, while every point on the chord produced lies outside—confirming that a straight line cannot cut a circle in more than two points.

15
00:01:40,659 --> 00:01:51,880
